[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a minimal, working example that colors a **circle** layer according to a numeric `population` property on each feature. The larger the population, the darker the circle (you can adjust the colors or the range to suit your data).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Population-based circle colors</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
    center: [-98, 38],
    zoom: 4
  });

  // Example GeoJSON – replace with your own source
  const cities = {
    type: "FeatureCollection",
    features: [
      { type: "Feature", properties: { name: "New York", population: 8419000 }, geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: [-74.006, 40.7128] } },
      { type: "Feature", properties: { name: "Los Angeles", population: 3980000 }, geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: [-118.2437, 34.0522] } },
      { type: "Feature", properties: { name: "Chicago", population: 2716000 }, geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: [-87.6298, 41.8781] } },
      // …more features…
    ]
  };

  map.on('load', () => {
    // Add the data source
    map.addSource('cities', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: cities
    });

    // Add the circle layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'city-circles',
      type: 'circle',
      source: 'cities',
      paint: {
        // Circle radius (optional – you can also base this on population)
        'circle-radius': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['get', 'population'],
          0,      2,   // tiny circles for very small values
          500000, 4,
          2000000, 8,
          10000000, 16   // biggest circles for the largest cities
        ],

        // Circle color – darker = larger population
        'circle-color': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['get', 'population'],
          //   value   color
          0,          '#fee5d9',   // light pink for the smallest
          500000,     '#fcae91',
          2000000,    '#fb6a4a',
          5000000,    '#de2d26',
          10000000,   '#a50f15'    // dark red for the biggest
        ],

        // Optional: make the circles semi-transparent so underlying basemap stays visible
        'circle-opacity': 0.7
      }
    });

    // (Optional) add a popup to show the name & population
    map.on('click', 'city-circles', (e) => {
      const props = e.features[0].properties;
      new maplibregl.Popup()
        .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
        .setHTML(`<strong>${props.name}</strong><br/>Population: ${props.population.toLocaleString()}`)
        .addTo(map);
    });

    // Change cursor on hover
    map.on('mouseenter', 'city-circles', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
    map.on('mouseleave', 'city-circles', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How the expression works

* **`['get', 'population']`** – pulls the numeric `population` property from each feature.
* **`['interpolate', ['linear'], …]`** – creates a piece-wise linear mapping from the numeric value to a visual value (color or radius).  
  * The first argument after `['linear']` is the input value (`population`).  
  * The following pairs are *stop* values: `value → output`.  
* The color stops go from a light hue (`#fee5d9`) for low populations to a dark hue (`#a50f15`) for high populations. Adjust the colors or add more stops to fine-tune the gradient.

### Tweaking it for your data

| What you want to change | How |
|--------------------------|-----|
| **Different color palette** | Replace the hex codes in the `circle-color` stops with any colors you like (e.g., blues, greens, or a sequential palette from ColorBrewer). |
| **More/less contrast** | Add or move stops. For a smoother gradient, add intermediate stops; for a sharper step-like effect, use `['step', …]` instead of `interpolate`. |
| **Base the radius on population** | The example already shows a radius interpolation. Adjust the stop values to match the range of your data. |
| **Use a logarithmic scale** | Replace `['linear']` with `['log']` (requires all values > 0). |
| **Clamp values** | Add `['clamp', …]` around the `['get', 'population']` if you want to force values outside a range to the nearest stop. |

### Common pitfalls

* **Property name mismatch** – Ensure the property you reference (`population` in the example) exactly matches the name in your source data (case-sensitive).  
* **Non-numeric values** – If some features have `null` or string values, the expression will evaluate to `null` and the circle will fall back to the default style. Clean or filter the data, or guard with `['coalesce', ['to-number', ['get', 'population']], 0]`.  
* **CORS / source loading** – The example uses an inline GeoJSON source, so CORS isn’t an issue. If you load a remote source, make sure the server sends proper CORS headers.

That’s it! With the `interpolate` expression on `circle-color` (and optionally `circle-radius`), larger population values will appear as darker circles on your MapLibre map. Adjust the stops and colors to match the visual language you need. Happy mapping!
